# Ana's postcard to her grandmother from the ferry town, read for who, what, when and where.
lesson language-postcard v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=5 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A postcard from the ferry town"
  goal "Read a postcard in [[language.name]] and answer questions about now, yesterday and tomorrow."

  look {
    scene 36x17 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        postcard card greeting="[[postcard.dear-grandma]]" text="[[postcard.ana-card]]" closing="[[postcard.love]]" from="Ana" to=["Rosa Díaz", "Calle Mayor 5", "Madrid"] stamp=ship date="3/8" lines=0
      }
    }
    say """
      Ana writes to her grandmother from the ferry town. A postcard starts with
      [[postcard.dear-grandma]], dear Grandma, and ends with [[postcard.love]], love. Read it for
      who is there, the weather, what Ana does now, what happened [[postcard.yesterday]] (yesterday)
      and what will happen [[postcard.tomorrow]] (tomorrow).
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.postcard-name k=3
    }
    show language.postcard-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.postcard-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.postcard-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.postcard-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.postcard-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.postcard-write k=5 easy-k=2 hard-k=3
    show language.postcard-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.postcard-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.postcard-write k=2
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-postcard-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.postcard-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A postcard: a greeting, where you are, the weather, what you do, and an ending."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is reading every sentence as now: the card has yesterday, today and
    tomorrow, and the three-star question asks for their order. The postcard is the same in every
    question. The writing piece is read by you: tick what you see on the list, and read it back
    aloud with the child. From this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word
    typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as
    Read aloud, or played by the device. If the child is stuck, underline yesterday, today and
    tomorrow on the card in three colours. Off the page, send a real postcard in the language. It
    meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "read
    carefully and show understanding of words, phrases and simple writing" and "write phrases from
    memory, and adapt these to create new sentences, to express ideas clearly"; Japan's grades 5 and
    6 foreign languages: "Can write about themselves and familiar and simple matters by using simple
    words and phrases and basic expressions for which they are sufficiently familiar with the sounds
    while refering to example sentences"; and CEFR A2, "Can describe in simple terms aspects of
    his/her background, immediate environment and matters in areas of immediate need". The phrases
    are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[postcard.ice-cream]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[postcard.with-her-parents]] and [[postcard.it-is-sunny]] and the words to write are [[postcard.sun]] and [[postcard.beach]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[postcard.dolphins]] and [[postcard.to-the-market]] and the words to write are [[postcard.grandmother]], [[postcard.ice-cream]] and [[postcard.beach]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
